const PERSEPHONE = { passage: 'Persephone and the Six Seeds\n\nPersephone was gathering narcissus flowers in a meadow when the ground split open. Out of the dark came a chariot pulled by black horses, and in it stood Hades, lord of the dead. Before she could scream for her mother, he had swept her down into the Underworld, and the earth closed over them like a mouth.\n\nHer mother was Demeter, goddess of the harvest. She searched for nine days without eating or sleeping, carrying a torch through every valley. When at last the Sun, who sees everything, told her the truth, her grief turned to fury. She left Olympus and walked the earth disguised as an old woman, and wherever she went, nothing grew. Wheat withered in the fields. Fruit shrivelled on the branch. People began to starve, and the gods, who loved the smoke of their offerings, grew afraid.\n\nZeus sent Hermes down to bring Persephone home. Hades did not argue. He only smiled, and offered her a pomegranate before she left. She had eaten nothing in the Underworld, but the journey was long, and the seeds glowed like rubies. She ate six.\n\nThose six seeds bound her. Anyone who eats the food of the dead must return to them. So it was agreed: for six months of every year Persephone lives with her mother, and the world blossoms; for six months she sits beside Hades as queen of the Underworld, and Demeter mourns, and the fields lie bare beneath the frost.\n\nThat, the Greeks said, is why we have winter.', passageSource: 'A Greek myth, retold for this app' };
const ORPHEUS = { passage: 'Orpheus Looks Back\n\nWhen Orpheus played his lyre, rivers paused to listen and wild animals lay down at his feet. When his wife Eurydice died from a snake bite soon after their wedding, he did what no living man had dared: he walked down into the Underworld to fetch her back.\n\nHe played for Charon, the ferryman, who carried him across the river for nothing. He played for Cerberus, the three-headed dog, and all three heads fell asleep. At last he stood before Hades and Persephone and sang of his loss, and for the first and only time, tears ran down the iron cheeks of the king of the dead.\n\n“Take her,” said Hades. “She will follow you up to the light. But you must not look back at her, not once, until you are both in the world above.”\n\nSo Orpheus climbed. The path was steep and silent. He could hear his own breathing, but not hers; he could hear his own footsteps, but not hers. Was she there? Had he been tricked? The doubt grew heavier with every step, until, with the sunlight already shining on his face, he could bear it no longer.\n\nHe turned.\n\nEurydice was there, one step behind him, still in the shadow. She reached out her hand, and as he reached for it she faded like mist, whispering a last farewell that he could barely hear.\n\nOrpheus never played a happy song again.', passageSource: 'A Greek myth, retold for this app' };
const ARACHNE = { passage: 'Arachne’s Tapestry\n\nIn a small town in Lydia lived a girl called Arachne, the finest weaver in the world. People travelled for days to watch her fingers fly across the loom. “Surely,” they said, “the goddess Athena herself taught you.”\n\n“No one taught me,” said Arachne. “I am better than Athena. Let her come and prove otherwise.”\n\nAthena came, disguised as an old woman, and gently warned her to ask the goddess’s forgiveness. Arachne laughed in her face. So the old woman threw off her disguise, and there stood Athena, grey-eyed and tall, and the contest began.\n\nAthena wove the gods of Olympus in their glory, and in each corner she showed a mortal who had challenged the gods and been punished. It was a warning, woven in thread.\n\nArachne ignored it. She wove the gods at their worst: tricking, lying, chasing and quarrelling. Her work was so perfect that the figures seemed to breathe. Even Athena could find no fault in it, and that, more than the insult, was what she could not bear. She tore the tapestry to pieces and struck Arachne with her shuttle.\n\nArachne’s body began to shrink. Her hair fell away. Her fingers grew long and thin and became legs, and her body became small and round. Athena had turned her into the first spider.\n\n“Live, then,” said the goddess, “and weave for ever.”\n\nAnd Arachne does. Look in any corner.', passageSource: 'A Greek myth (as told by the Roman poet Ovid), retold for this app' };

// --- an original short story (Mira, Theo and Kit) ------------------------------------------
const STEP = { passage: 'The Forty-Fifth Step\n\nMira counted the steps of the British Museum every time she came, and there were always forty-four. Today there were forty-five.\n\nShe stopped. Her brother Theo walked straight into her and dropped his sketchbook. Their friend Kit, who never noticed anything that was not on a screen, kept going until the shadow of the great columns swallowed him.\n\n“There’s an extra step,” Mira said.\n\n“There’s an extra sister,” said Theo, picking up his pencils. “One who counts things.”\n\nInside, the Greek galleries were cool and almost empty. A guard with silver hair and a grey cardigan sat beside the case of painted pots, reading a paperback so old its cover had worn white. She looked up as they passed, and Mira had the odd feeling of being weighed, like fruit on a market stall. On the guard’s collar was a small brooch: an owl.\n\nThe pot Mira loved best showed a woman at a loom. “Arachne,” said Kit, reading the label on his phone. “She beat Athena at weaving. Legend.”\n\n“She didn’t beat her,” said the guard, without looking up. “She was punished for being proud. Different thing.” She turned a page. “Though, between you and me, her work was better.”\n\nKit laughed. Mira didn’t. The guard’s eyes were grey, the clear, cold grey of a winter sea, and they were looking at Mira now.\n\n“You count things,” said the guard. “Good. Most people only look.”\n\nWhen Mira glanced back from the doorway, the chair was empty. The paperback lay closed on the seat.\n\nOutside, the light was already fading. Mira counted the steps on the way down. Forty-four.\n\nOn the place where the forty-fifth had been lay a single grey feather.', passageSource: 'A short story written for this app' };

